# os -- process-level primitives. Paired with `io` for file/stream
# operations; where `io` surfaces failures as `result(T, IoError)`,
# `os` primitives are either infallible (exit) or use `optionval`
# when the value may simply be absent (env).

# Public interface -- the definitions other units may reference; all other
# top-level definitions are private to this unit.
public: unit {
    :args
    :env
    :setEnv
    :unsetEnv
    :envNames
    :cwd
    :exePath
    :setCwd
    :cpuCount
    :pid
    :ppid
    :monotonicMillis
    :spawn
    :procexit
    :Child
    :spawnAsync
    :waitAny
    :sleepMillis
    :userName
    :homeDir
    :tempDir
    :nullDevice
    :hostOsName
    :hostArchName
    :buildCommit
    :buildDate
    :buildCompiler
    :hostname
    :exit
}

# args -- program argument vector as passed to `main`. Position 0 is
# the program name (as the kernel / shell provided it); subsequent
# elements are the explicit arguments. The returned list is a
# freshly-allocated value; the caller owns it and the heap strings
# inside, so scope exit frees both.
args: function out List String is native

# env -- look up environment variable `key`. Returns
# `optionval.some value` when the variable is set (even to the empty
# string); `optionval.none` when the variable is not present in the
# current environment. Missing-env is not modeled as an IoError
# because it's an expected everyday condition, not a failure.
env: function {key: StringView} out Option String is native

# setEnv -- insert or overwrite an environment variable in the
# current process. Returns `ok null` on success; `err IoError` when
# libc refuses the call (invalid name, out of memory). Reuses the
# `io.IoError` surface because the underlying errno domain is the
# same and callers often interleave env mutation with file I/O. In a
# program that starts threads it is refused (`IoError.other`) while
# another thread runs.
setEnv: function {key: StringView value: StringView} out (Result null E: IoError) is native

# unsetEnv -- remove `key` from the environment. Succeeds (`ok null`)
# whether or not `key` was previously set; errors only on an invalid
# key name (EINVAL surfaces as `IoError.other`). Refused while
# another thread runs, as setEnv is.
unsetEnv: function {key: StringView} out (Result null E: IoError) is native

# envNames -- snapshot of every environment variable name in the
# current process, in `environ` order. The returned list and each of
# its entries are freshly-allocated; scope exit frees both. Callers
# typically iterate and use `env` to fetch each value; omitting
# values from the snapshot avoids an O(n) string copy for callers
# that filter first.
envNames: function out List String is native

# cwd -- current working directory as an absolute path. Typically
# succeeds; errors only when the cwd inode has been unlinked or
# permission to resolve it has been revoked.
cwd: function out (Result String E: IoError) is native

# exePath -- absolute path of the running executable (Linux reads
# /proc/self/exe; macOS uses _NSGetExecutablePath; Windows
# GetModuleFileNameW, a native path). Lets a program locate its own install
# tree. Errors only if reading the link fails.
exePath: function out (Result String E: IoError) is native

# setCwd -- chdir to `path`. Affects subsequent relative-path io
# calls -- in every thread: the working directory is the process's, not a
# thread's. Fails with the usual IoError variants (notfound, notdir,
# permissiondenied).
setCwd: function {path: StringView} out (Result null E: IoError) is native

# cpuCount -- online processors. For sizing a worker pool: a harness that
# hardcodes a width is right on one machine and wrong everywhere else. Never
# returns less than 1, so a caller can divide by it; a host that cannot answer
# reads as 1, which means "run serially" rather than "run zero jobs".
cpuCount: function out i32 is native

# pid -- current process id. pid_t is `int` on Linux; we narrow to
# i32. Infallible.
pid: function out i32 is native

# ppid -- parent process id. Infallible. 0 where the system records none
ppid: function out i32 is native

# monotonicMillis -- milliseconds on the monotonic clock. Only
# differences are meaningful (the epoch is arbitrary). Infallible.
monotonicMillis: function out i64 is native

# spawn -- start `argv` as a child process and wait for it. `argv[0]` is
# resolved via PATH; on Windows a name without an extension is tried with .exe
# and .com, and a .bat or .cmd file is refused -- cmd.exe runs those, so spawn
# `cmd /c <file>` explicitly. The child's stdin is the null device (/dev/null,
# NUL on Windows); its stdout goes to `outPath` and stderr to `errPath`, each
# created or truncated (an empty path inherits this process's stream; empty
# both inherits stdin too). A
# non-empty `cwd` is the child's working directory. The redirection files are
# opened in the parent before the child starts, so its cwd cannot change their
# resolution, and no other child inherits them: a child sees only its three
# standard streams. Returns the child's exit status; 128 + signo for a child a
# signal ended; 127 if it could not be started; 124 if it outlived
# `timeoutSecs` > 0 and was killed (0 waits indefinitely). Read captured output
# back with `io.readText`; set the child's environment via `os.setEnv`
# beforehand (it is inherited).
spawn: function {
    # the program and its arguments; argv[0] is the program
    argv: List String
    # the directory the child starts in; empty keeps this process's own
    cwd: StringView
    # the file the child's stdout is redirected to; /dev/null discards it
    outPath: StringView
    # the file the child's stderr is redirected to
    errPath: StringView
    # seconds before the child is killed, answering 124 as its status; 0 waits
    # forever
    timeoutSecs: i32
} out i32 is native

# procexit -- one reaped child: its pid and the decoded exit status
# (spawn's conventions: the exit code; 128 + signo for a child a signal ended;
# 137 for one `kill` ended, on every system).
procexit: record {
    # the child waited for; 0 when none had exited
    pid: i32
    # its exit status
    status: i32
}

# Child -- a child process `spawnAsync` started: its pid, and the operating
# system's handle on that very process (a pidfd on Linux where the kernel has
# them, the process handle on Windows), so a `kill` can never reach another
# process that came to reuse the pid. Dropping a Child closes the handle and
# leaves the process running; `waitAny` reaps it.
Child: class {
    # its process id
    pid: i32
    # the operating system's handle on the process: a pidfd or -1 on POSIX,
    # the process HANDLE on Windows
    handle: i64
    # whether it holds the handle: false in a Child whose value moved out,
    # which is all zeros, so its destructor releases nothing
    open: bool
    # whether waitAny has reaped it, after which kill does nothing
    reaped: bool
} as {
    # kill -- end the child now (SIGKILL; TerminateProcess on Windows, whose
    # exit status is then 137, as a SIGKILLed child's is). Nothing once it is
    # reaped; reap it with `waitAny` afterwards.
    kill: function {:this} is native
}

# spawnAsync -- start `argv` like `spawn` but return immediately with the
# Child. Redirection and cwd behave exactly as in `spawn`; there is no timeout
# -- reap with `waitAny` and enforce deadlines with `monotonicMillis` and
# `Child.kill`. The error says why the start failed: a program that is not
# there is `notfound`.
spawnAsync: function {
    # the program and its arguments; argv[0] is the program
    argv: (List String).view
    # the directory the child starts in; empty keeps this process's own
    cwd: StringView
    # the file the child's stdout is redirected to
    outPath: StringView
    # the file the child's stderr is redirected to
    errPath: StringView
} out (Result Child E: IoError) is native

# waitAny -- reap one exited child of `children` without blocking, marking it
# reaped: pid 0 when none of them has exited yet. Only the children given are
# reaped -- never another part of the program's -- and one already reaped is
# skipped.
waitAny: function {
    # the children to wait for
    children: List Child
} out procexit is native

# sleepMillis -- suspend the calling thread for `ms` milliseconds.
sleepMillis: function {ms: i64} is native

# userName -- login name of the effective user, resolved via the
# passwd database (getpwuid_r(geteuid())), or on Windows the account the
# process runs as (GetUserNameW). Errors when the uid has no passwd entry
# or the database is unreadable.
userName: function out (Result String E: IoError) is native

# homeDir -- the effective user's home directory. Reads $HOME from the
# environment (%USERPROFILE% on Windows); errors with `IoError.other` when it
# is unset. Does not fall back to the passwd database — set the variable
# explicitly if you need portable behavior across stripped environments.
homeDir: function out (Result String E: IoError) is native

# tempDir -- the directory for temporary files: $TMPDIR, else /tmp; on
# Windows GetTempPathW's (%TMP%, %TEMP%, %USERPROFILE%, else the Windows
# directory), a native path. Never ends in a separator but at a root.
tempDir: function out String is native

# nullDevice -- the device that discards what is written to it and reads as
# empty: "/dev/null", or "NUL" on Windows
nullDevice: function out String is {
    if z.build.os == z.build.oskind.windows then return "NUL".string
    return "/dev/null".string
}

# hostOsName -- the OS family this binary was compiled for, as one of
# "linux" / "darwin" / "windows" / "other". A NAME rather than a variant so
# nothing in the runtime has to know the generated `z` unit's kinds; the
# compiler writes the matching `z.build.oskind` arm into that unit. For the
# compile-time answer -- what the build is FOR, which a cross build makes
# different -- read `z.build.os` instead.
hostOsName: function out String is native

# hostArchName -- the CPU family, as "x86_64" / "aarch64" / "other".
hostArchName: function out String is native

# buildCommit -- the build stamp this binary carries, or the empty string
# when its build supplied none. A build sets it by defining ZC_BUILD_COMMIT
# for the C compiler (`zc build --cflags`) or by linking a strong
# `z_build_commit` symbol.
buildCommit: function out String is native

# buildDate -- companion to `buildCommit`: the date its build stamped in,
# or the empty string.
buildDate: function out String is native

# buildCompiler -- name and version of the C compiler that built this
# binary, such as "gcc 15.2.0". Always present: it comes from that
# compiler's own predefined macros, not from the build.
buildCompiler: function out String is native

# hostname -- result of `gethostname(2)`, truncated at HOST_NAME_MAX; on
# Windows the DNS host name (GetComputerNameExW). Result-wrapped because the
# syscall can fail in rare cases.
hostname: function out (Result String E: IoError) is native

# exit -- terminate the process immediately with `code` as the
# status. Does not return; buffered output is flushed, but any
# zerolang-level destructors for in-scope values DO NOT run. In a
# program that starts threads the process ends once: a thread that
# exits or panics while another is already ending it waits for that
# end. Reserve for fatal-error exits and CLI tools that want an
# explicit status; prefer a normal return path for everyday success
# / failure.
exit: function {code: i32} is native
